Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.960
Having a world record is a pretty great&nbsp;
achievement. Having a world record that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:11.760
mathematically unbeatable is even better. Today&nbsp;
I'm going to show you some world record surfaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:18.960
which are part of the field of algebraic geometry.&nbsp;
That topic is really a way to connect equations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:25.200
with the beautiful shapes that they correspond to.&nbsp;
Mathematicians have always been eager to visualise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.280
their work and get an idea of what an equation&nbsp;
looks like. Without computers around, they went&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:38.720
to great lengths to calculate lots of values, and&nbsp;
used plaster, wire and paper to handcraft those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:45.760
models. World Record surfaces are particularly&nbsp;
baffling shapes that stand out because of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:52.160
number of singularities. I'll get to what that&nbsp;
means in just a bit. But first, let's take a look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:01:02.320
at how an equation forms any shape at all. This&nbsp;
is a free programme called SURFER,derived from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.760
the word surface, there's a link to it down in the&nbsp;
description. It allows us to visualise the shapes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:14.880
from equations in real time. More precisely, those&nbsp;
equations are polynomials in the three variables&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:21.840
x, y, and z. We don't have to worry about&nbsp;
logarithms, roots, or negative exponents here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:28.640
algebraic geometry cares about sufficiently nice&nbsp;
equations. Let's take a look at the equation x&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:35.360
squared plus y squared plus z squared minus one&nbsp;
is equal to zero. Having Pythagoras in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:41.920
the solution here are just those points that are&nbsp;
a distance of one from the origin. And indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:48.320
we can see a perfect sphere on our screen. If I&nbsp;
rotate the sphere around, you can't even notice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.720
This is because of its perfect symmetry. And&nbsp;
we can see that the symmetry is anchored in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.720 --> 00:02:02.400
equation already, we do the same thing to x, y and&nbsp;
z, which is raising the variable to the power of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:08.880
two and adding them all up. We can't distinguish&nbsp;
between the variables in terms of mathematical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:15.760
operations, so we wouldn't expect to see anything&nbsp;
unsymmetric in this picture. But let's try and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:23.920
break the symmetry, we can keep x squared plus&nbsp;
y squared, and just write minus z squared. So we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:30.560
still can't differ between x and y. But something&nbsp;
should happen in the z direction. And lucky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:37.920
enough, that's exactly what we see. This double&nbsp;
cone is still symmetric in the x and y directions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:44.240
But we see a different story when we look up and&nbsp;
down. But there is something else going on here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:51.520
You can see that very spiky point in the middle.&nbsp;
Well, this is what we refer to as a singularity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.160
If the first thing that pops into your&nbsp;
mind, upon hearing, the word Singularity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.840
is a black hole or the movie Interstellar, then&nbsp;
you're on a great path to becoming a physicist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.680
In more abstract mathematics. This term could mean&nbsp;
a lot of different things, and usually describes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.640
points where something interesting happens. It&nbsp;
is more than good enough for this video to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:20.560
thinking of that double cone intersection when&nbsp;
you hear the word singularity. For those of you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:26.240
eager for a more technical definition, these&nbsp;
points have vanishing partial derivatives.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:32.160
Now, how do these singularities relate to the&nbsp;
world records we spoke about before? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:37.680
it wouldn't really make sense to just try&nbsp;
and find the most amount of singularities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.640
because there wouldn't really be a limit&nbsp;
and instead what is quite a natural question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:49.760
is to ask how many singularities can you find&nbsp;
for a given degree of a polynomial equation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.960
The degree just counts how many&nbsp;
variables get multiplied together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:01.920
in each term of the equation. For example,&nbsp;
the degree of our sphere, or our double cone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:10.800
would be two. Since two variables get multiplied&nbsp;
in the terms, the degree of x times y times z&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:17.360
equals zero, would therefore be three. If two&nbsp;
terms have different amounts of those variables,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:23.600
the degree would be the bigger number. So&nbsp;
x squared times y times z plus z squared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:31.040
has a degree of four, and so on. As it turns out,&nbsp;
the double cone is already a world record surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:37.120
itself. As it turns out that you cannot have&nbsp;
more than one singularity for degree two. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:43.040
what about higher degrees? Mathematicians asked&nbsp;
themselves this exact question for centuries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:49.920
And yet we still only know the exact number of&nbsp;
singularities up to degree six. So there are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:55.520
lots of records still to be snatched out there.&nbsp;
And it might just be one of you watching now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:02.880
he gets one. Mathematicians have been able to sort&nbsp;
of sandwich the maximum number of singularities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:09.520
a surface of a given degree could have by&nbsp;
examples for the lower bound, and some more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:16.160
abstract arguments for the upper bound. And it's&nbsp;
finally time to get to the star of this video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:24.080
a surface of degree six, simply called a sextic&nbsp;
that cannot be bettered, the Barth Sextic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:34.640
Six is currently the highest degree for which&nbsp;
an unbeatable record exists. And the history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:40.560
of the surface is quite interesting. It's a&nbsp;
surface that really shouldn't be possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:50.640
Here are two research papers by well known Italian&nbsp;
mathematicians, one from 1946, and one from 1982.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:56.080
They both went through a thorough review process&nbsp;
for the reputable journals they will published.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:04.560
The older paper claims that the maximum number of&nbsp;
singularities for a sextic is 52. While the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:12.880
one claims it is 64. Yet they are both wrong.&nbsp;
They're wrong because the Barth Sextic published&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:22.720
in 1996, 16 years after the second paper actually&nbsp;
has 65 singularities. One is quick to think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:29.680
something mathematically proven would outlast&nbsp;
any debate. However, since we're human after all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:37.200
we consider a mathematical proof correct if we&nbsp;
don't find any mistakes in it. In the same year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:45.040
as Barth introduced his new shape to the world,&nbsp;
two other researchers proved that 66 singularities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:51.440
wasn't possible, making Barth's record unbeatable,&nbsp;
at least if there are no mistakes this time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

